$DIR/main.p:17:3: defer in a loop: a path from it can reach it again without leaving the loop (a defer statement runs at most once in each call of the function); move the loop body into a function
    A defer statement in a loop would add a deferred call on every iteration,
    and each needs room in the function's stack frame until it returns.
    Plutonium gives every deferred call a fixed slot in the frame, so a defer
    statement may run at most once in each call of the function.
    This defer is allowed in a loop only if every path from it leaves every loop
    that contains it before it can reach the defer again. A path leaves by
    return, by a call of panic or of a function that never returns, by goto to a
    label outside the loop, by a break that ends the loop, or by a labeled
    continue of a loop outside it. An unlabeled continue does not leave. The
    rule is the same for a for statement, a range statement, the body of a loop
    over a function (for x := range seq) and a loop made by a backward goto.
    If the deferred call should run at the end of each iteration, move the loop
    body into a function of its own; its defer then runs at the end of each
    iteration.
    Go keeps the deferred calls of a loop in a list on the heap. Plutonium never
    allocates behind the program's back, and kernel stacks are small and fixed.
    Move the body into a function:
        for _, name := range names {
            process(name)
        }

        func process(name string) {
            f := open(name)
            defer f.Close()
            ...
        }
    (see "Defer statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.19)
    (run "pluto explain E2530" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:24:10: a function literal deferred in a range-over-func loop body cannot capture x, a variable of the loop body: the deferred call runs after the body has returned
    A function literal deferred in the body of a loop over a function runs when
    the enclosing function returns, after the loop body has returned. The body's
    own variables, including the loop variables, live in the body's frame, which
    is gone by then, so the literal cannot capture them.
    Pass the values the deferred call needs as arguments: arguments are
    evaluated when the defer statement runs.
    Go moves such variables to the heap. Plutonium never moves a variable to the
    heap; a closure is valid only while the frame it captures from is.
    Pass the loop variable as an argument:
        for x := range seq {
            if done(x) {
                defer func(x int) { log(x) }(x)
                return
            }
        }
    (see "Range over functions" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.11)
    (run "pluto explain E2534" for a longer explanation)
